    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d174">

                <pb facs="0419" n="295" sameAs="frus:frus1881/pg295"/>

                <head>No. 174.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Blaine to Mr. Angell</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1881-08-26"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 26,
                        1881</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 122.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 174, of June 20 last,
                    relative to the exclusive privileges which the Great Northern Telegraph Company
                    are desirous <pb facs="0420" n="296" xml:id="pg296"/> of securing from China,
                    has been received and read with interest and your action respecting the subject,
                    is approved.</p>
                <p>In addition to your forcible presenting of the case and the evils not unlikely to
                    result from such a course as the one proposed, you may call the attention of the
                    Chinese authorities to the fact that the granting of monopolies of any kind is
                    not conducive to public interests, and endeavor to dissuade them from giving
                    their assent to any contracts like the one mentioned in your dispatch.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>JAMES G. BLAINE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
